    "product_id": "595d7c22-f48e-42ec-96b9-b076cf6be467",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 181127\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n427 AM MST Sun Oct 18 2026\n\n.UPDATE...Updated 12z Aviation Discussion...\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Chances for light rain will heighten over Arizona this weekend \nwith the best chances for accumulations focused over higher terrain \nlocations. \n\n- Temperatures will gradually warm closer to the seasonal normal \nthrough the middle of next week.\n  \n- A Pacific low pressure system moving into the Southwest during the \nmiddle and end of next week will result in much better rainfall \nchances with the possibility of a few embedded thunderstorms, as \nwell as cooler temperatures \n\n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /Today through Monday/... \nThe Rex block pattern that was positioned over west CONUS has \nalready begun to break down as the high pressure is now over the \nNorthern Plain States. The high pressure will continue to quickly \nmove into NE CONUS by late this evening. Meanwhile the cut off low\nto our SW has already begun to loosen into an open wave. As the \nlow continues to open and begin to be absorbed into the main \nwesterly flow aloft decent moisture advection through the mid \nlevels will occur, leading to PWATS in excess of 1\" across much of\nthe CWA. This is near 200-225% of normal, or above the 90th \npercentile of NAEFS mean precipitable water, very unusual for this\ntime of year. Additionally, as the low opens it will place south \ncentral AZ in a favorable position for associated positive \nvorticity, aiding in the ascent of the aforementioned moisture. As\na result slight chances for isolated showers will continue to \nlinger, with PoPs between 15-30% stretching between southern \nMaricopa County through Pinal County and into the higher terrain \neast of the Phoenix metro through the morning hours into the \nafternoon. By this evening, the upper low will finally begin \nejecting into northern Arizona, but even so, moist upslope through\nGila County could still support a couple isolated light showers \ninto Monday morning. By Monday afternoon most of the lower deserts\nPoPs are <10% while 20-30% persist over the eastern high terrain,\nhowever if anything does develop, total accumulations are \nexpected to be minimal. \n\nLarge spread in NBM temperatures remain for today and Monday with \nthe uncertainty of showers, and depth of H5 heights associated with \nthe low. Regardless, high temperatures today are forecasted to be in \nthe mid 80s across the lower deserts. By Monday, ahead of the next \nlow pressure system, H5 heights will increase as a ridge builds over \neastern CONUS, in turn heights aloft increase slightly over the \nDesert SW raising temperatures a few degrees into the mid to upper 80s \nthrough mid-week. \n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /Tuesday through Saturday/... \nBy Tuesday, deep troughing and strong negative height anomalies \nwill amplify and intensify along the west coast, however \nconfidence in the timing and depth of this feature continues to be\nlow to moderate. The deterministic's have started to come into \nbetter agreement compared to 24 hrs ago but still show key \ndifferences. The ECMWF forecast continues to show a slightly \nslower system however much shallower than before. Meanwhile the \nGFS deterministic remains mostly unchanged, with the exception of \nnow leaning towards a more positively tilted trough than compared \nto the ECMWF, and the previous GFS runs, spreading out any \nvorticity advection and hindering better chances for storm \ndevelopment. Even with these differences compared to previous \nruns, the ECMWF continues to forecast a more more robust rainfall \nscenario in comparison to the GFS. Either way, Wednesday and \nThursday continue to be the best window for rainfall potential, \nwhile pattern recognition and some instability parameters suggest \nthe potential for strong thunderstorms ahead of the shortwave \npassage. Lingering PoPs continue into Friday morning before \nexpecting to dry out by the weekend. \n\nAdditionally, with the uncertainty of this next system remaining,\na wide spread in NBM temperatures continue throughout the week, \nmost notably on Thursday with a spread of 11 degrees between the \n25th and 75th percentile. Even with this large of spread below \nnormal highs are likely late this week heading into the weekend.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 1120Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT: \nNo major aviation weather concerns are expected throughout the TAF\nperiod. Wind directions will maintain an easterly component\nthroughout the period with speeds generally aob 10 kts. VCSH will\nremain a threat through the mid-morning hours, however,\noperational impacts should remain minimal. Abundant mid-level \nclouds will remain in place through the morning hours before \nscattering out heading into the afternoon hours. \n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH:\nNo major aviation concerns are expected throughout the TAF period.\nAt KIPL, winds will generally maintain a westerly component while\nat KBLH winds will fluctuate between the north-northwest to\nnortheast. Overall wind speeds will generally be aob 6 kts along \nwith extended periods of light and variable conditions. SCT mid- \nlevel cloud decks early this morning will give way to mostly clear\nskies by the mid to late morning hours.\n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nWind speeds through the beginning of next week will be much weaker \nthan the past several days with minimal gustiness. Light showers \nwill affect much of the Arizona districts this weekend, and \nparticularly over eastern districts, but the chance for wetting rain \nis low (~10%). Minimum humidity levels will improve closer to a 30-\n50% range during the first half of next week, then likely rise \nfurther during the latter half of the week. Overnight recovery \nshould be good to excellent. A Pacific weather disturbance will \nbring increased chances of wetting rainfall, breezy winds, and \ncooler temperatures during the latter half of next week.\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...None.\nCA...None.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Ryan\nLONG TERM...Ryan \nAVIATION...Lojero\nFIRE WEATHER...18/Ryan\n",
